<<<
are:—({preferring broad) expanse, brow, forehead, lands, estates, acres, brim, mind, gauge; (preferring wide) opening, gap, gulf, culture.
===
are:—(preferring *broad*) *expanse*, *brow*, *forehead*, *lands*, *estates*, *acres*, *brim*, *mind*, *gauge*; (preferring *wide*) *opening*, *gap*, *gulf*, *culture*.
>>>

<<<
(8) Some illustrations of the difference in meaning between broad & wide with the same word; the first two may be thought fanciful, but hardly the others: A w. door is one that gives entrance to several abreast, a 6. door one of imposing dimensions;
===
(3) Some illustrations of the difference in meaning between *broad* & *wide* with the same word; the first two may be thought fanciful, but hardly the others: *A w. door* is one that gives entrance to several abreast, *a b. door* one of imposing dimensions;
>>>

<<<
@ w. river takes long to cross, a b. rwer shows a fine expanse of water; a@ @. generalization covers - many particulars, a b. generalization disregards unimportant exceptions;
===
*a w. river* takes long to cross, *a b. river* shows a fine expanse of water; *a w. generalization* covers many particulars, *a b. generalization* disregards unimportant exceptions;
>>>

<<<
a page has a b. margin, i.e. a fine expanse of white, but we allow a w. margin for extras, i.e. a great interval between the certain & the possible costs; @ w. distinction or difference implies that the things are very far from identical, but a 6. distinction or difference is merely one that requires no subtlety for its appreciation.
===
a page has *a b. margin*, i.e. a fine expanse of white, but we allow *a w. margin* for extras, i.e. a great interval between the certain & the possible costs; *a w. distinction* or *difference* implies that the things are very far from identical, but *a b. distinction* or *difference* is merely one that requires no subtlety for its appreciation.
>>>

<<<
broadness is now used instead of the usual breadth only when the meaning is coarseness or indelicacy of expression.
===
**broadness** is now used instead of the usual *breadth* only when the meaning is coarseness or indelicacy of expression.
>>>

<<<
Brobdingnag (not -ignag) is the spelling.
===
**Brobdingnag** (not *-ignag*) is the spelling.
>>>

<<<
broccoli (not -oco-, nor -lo) is the best spelling. The word is an Italian plural, & is generally used collectively like spinach &c.; but if a or the plural is wanted, a broccoli, two broccolis, are the forms.
===
**broccoli** (not *-oco-*, nor *-lo*) is the best spelling. The word is an Italian plural, & is generally used collectively like *spinach* &c.; but if *a* or the plural is wanted, *a broccoli*, *two broccolis*, are the forms.
>>>

<<<
brochure, pamphlet. See FRENCH worps. 8B. has no right to exist in English, since it is not needed by the side of p. Its introduction in the 19th c. was probably due to misconception of the French uses. In French 6b. is used where the French p. (chiefly applied to scurrilous or libellous or violently controversial pamphlets) is inappropriate.
===
**brochure, pamphlet.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^. *B.* has no right to exist in English, since it is not needed by the side of *p.* Its introduction in the 19th c. was probably due to misconception of the French uses. In French *b.* is used where the French *p.* (chiefly applied to scurrilous or libellous or violently controversial pamphlets) is inappropriate.
>>>

<<<
The sense *a few leaves of printed matter stitched together ’ has always belonged in English to p., though it has by the side of this general sense the special one (different from the French) ‘ p. bearing on some question
===
The sense 'a few leaves of printed matter stitched together' has always belonged in English to *p.*, though it has by the side of this general sense the special one (different from the French) 'p. bearing on some question
>>>

<<<
+ of current interest (esp. in politics or theology)’. ‘° Dans sa brochure appelde en anglais pamphlet’, quoted in French dictionaries from Voltaire, gives us the useful hint that the English for 6. is p.
===
+ of current interest (esp. in politics or theology)'. 'Dans sa brochure appelée en anglais *pamphlet*', quoted in French dictionaries from Voltaire, gives us the useful hint that the English for *b.* is *p.*
>>>

